{
  "teamId": 133,
  "year": 2025,
  "narrative": "The 2025 Oakland Athletics arrived at their new home in Sacramento with cautious optimism, and early April offered a genuine reason for excitement. Jacob Wilson announced himself as a future cornerstone with a clutch walk-off single on April 4th, driving in two runs to deliver one of the season's most electric moments. For a brief window, it felt like this young roster might exceed expectations.\n\nBut the hope dissolved quickly. A brutal stretch from late April through early June saw Oakland hemorrhage leads and absorb demoralizing blows, most painfully a Luis Urías home run that wiped out a late-game cushion and a grand slam surrendered by Mason Miller that crystallized the team's pitching vulnerabilities. The A's ERA of 4.70 was not merely a number — it was the sound of rallies extinguished and games surrendered in the final innings. By the time June arrived, Oakland was firmly below .500 and fading from any realistic playoff conversation.\n\nThe summer months offered scattered flickers of life. Willie MacIver's clutch August double sparked a late-game comeback, and Shea Langeliers — who would finish with 29 home runs — delivered timely blasts that kept the team from completely collapsing. The offense, built around 219 home runs and a .749 OPS, had real teeth, and on good nights the A's could beat anyone. But for every Langeliers moonshot, there was a grand slam allowed or a late-inning unraveling that undid the work.\n\nSeptember brought the arithmetic of elimination into full view. Oakland finished 76-86, outscored by 84 runs on the season, and missed the playoffs comfortably. The closing weeks had a valedictory feel — young players logging experience, veterans playing out strings, and a fanbase left to wonder whether the talent accumulating in the system could eventually match the promise of Jacob Wilson's very first swing of the year.",
